","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the input is made of.** Every input contains one full line of colour 1 along an edge, plus many identical 3×3 hollow squares. The squares sit on a regular lattice with step 4, so they fill a 7×7 grid of slots, and each slot is either empty or holds a square of some colour. One middle lattice line is always completely empty:\n- In pairs 1 and 3, the line of 1 is horizontal and the middle column of slots is empty.\n- In pair 2, the line of 1 is vertical and the middle row of slots is empty.\n\n**Building the output.**\n1. Turn each square into a single cell, giving a 7×7 summary grid.\n2. Delete the empty middle line. This gives 7×6 in pairs 1 and 3, and 6×7 in pair 2, which matches the output sizes.\n3. Slide the cells in each line toward one side, keeping their order. Which side depends on where the 1-line was:\n   - Pair 1: the line is on top. Lattice row 1 holds one 2 and one 8, and the output row is `0 0 0 0 2 8`, so cells move **right**.\n   - Pair 3: the line is at the bottom. Lattice row 0 holds 8 · · · 9 · 9, and the output row is `8 9 9 0 0 0`, so cells move **left**.\n   - Pair 2: the line is on the right. Lattice column 0 reads 6,6,·,·,8,8,8 from top to bottom, and the output column is `0,6,6,8,8,8`, so cells move **down**.\n\n   The pattern is that the push direction is the 1-line's side turned 90° clockwise: top→right, right→down, bottom→left, left→up.\n\n**Test.** The 1-line is on the left, so the cells will move **up**. The empty lattice line is row 3 (y=13), which gets removed. Each of the 7 columns of the resulting 6×7 grid is then compacted upward, with the 8s from the upper half followed by the 2s/7s from the lower half.\n\nThe program emits three stages: the lattice summary, the summary with the empty line removed, and the packed result.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # locate the colour-1 line and which side it is on\n    side = None\n    for y in (0, H - 1):\n        if len(set(g[y].tolist())) == 1 and g[y, 0] != 0:\n            side = 'top' if y == 0 else 'bottom'\n            lc = g[y, 0]\n    for x in (0, W - 1):\n        if len(set(g[:, x].tolist())) == 1 and g[0, x] != 0:\n            side = 'left' if x == 0 else 'right'\n            lc = g[0, x]\n    objs = [o for o in objects(g, bg=0) if o[\"size\"] == 8]\n    ry = objs[0][\"box\"][0] % 4\n    rx = objs[0][\"box\"][1] % 4\n    lat = np.zeros((7, 7), dtype=int)\n    for o in objs:\n        y0, x0 = o[\"box\"][0], o[\"box\"][1]\n        lat[(y0 - ry) // 4, (x0 - rx) // 4] = o[\"colour\"]\n    emit(lat.copy(), \"Summarise each 3x3 hollow square as one cell of a 7x7 lattice.\")\n    if side in ('top', 'bottom'):\n        red = np.delete(lat, 3, axis=1)\n    else:\n        red = np.delete(lat, 3, axis=0)\n    emit(red.copy(), \"Remove the empty middle line of the lattice.\")\n    out = np.zeros_like(red)\n    h, w = red.shape\n    if side in ('top', 'bottom'):\n        for r in range(h):\n            items = [v for v in red[r] if v != 0]\n            if side == 'top':   # push right\n                out[r, w - len(items):] = items\n            else:               # push left\n                out[r, :len(items)] = items\n    else:\n        for c in range(w):\n            items = [v for v in red[:, c] if v != 0]\n            if side == 'right':  # push down\n                out[h - len(items):, c] = items\n            else:                # push up\n                out[:len(items), c] = items\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Slide the cells in each line toward the side given by the 1-line rotated clockwise.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide the cells in each line toward the side given by the 1-line rotated clockwise."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Slide the cells in each line toward one side, keeping their order."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide the cells in each line toward the side given by the 1-line rotated clockwise."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The pattern is that the push direction is the 1-line's side turned 90° clockwise: top→right, right→down, bottom→left, left→up."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"This gives 7×6 in pairs 1 and 3, and 6×7 in pair 2, which matches the output sizes."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 1: the line is on top."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"7afb7d18b90b10390cc631e3371c2bfb1895b383c3ea4c28bd2ecda20b215e5c","task_sha256":"2feb3735308e24b73d9c32b6a8efdbe7852561ae88b6ed33255394f20c6ba1ae","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
